          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think It Over"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.072,
            0.644,
            0.255,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Water can be obtained from various sources. Are all these samples of water chemically identical?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.073,
            0.739,
            0.259,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Oxygen is sometimes represented as O and sometimes as O . What is the difference between these symbols?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.073,
            0.834,
            0.259,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why does dissolved salt in water conduct electricity, but sugar does not?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.284,
            0.607,
            0.89,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Chapter 8, Journey Inside the Atom, you explored the structure of the atom. You also studied subatomic particles, viz., electrons, protons and neutrons, in terms of their discoveries, properties and locations in the atom. You also learnt that atoms with an octet of electrons in their valence shell are stable. Atoms can lose, gain or share electrons to achieve an octet of valence electrons."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.71,
            0.891,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have also learnt that many properties of the Grade 8 Curiosity elements are not retained when they form a compound. Chapter 8 For example, hydrogen and oxygen are gases, whereas the substance formed when they combine is water, which is a liquid at ordinary temperatures."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.795,
            0.89,
            0.916
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Interestingly, water does not have the same properties as hydrogen or oxygen. Hydrogen gas is combustible and oxygen supports combustion, whereas water neither burns nor helps in burning; rather, it extinguishes fire. However, it was found that the mass of the water formed equals to the sum of the masses of the hydrogen and oxygen that combined to form it. Let us "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "explore",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " whether the mass remains unchanged during physical and chemical changes."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
